{"id":218651595932715,"type":"song","name":"Charlie Brown","artist":"The Coasters","year":1959,"weight":17,"info":{"cover":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/cover/b99856ee3d94d73940b5e632ad0f41f8/250x250-000000-80-0-0.jpg","album":"The Coasters - The Coasters - Poison Ivy (MP3 EP)","yt":"-D0SUGdO4ok"},"artist_ref":{"id":243657217553935,"name":"The Coasters","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Coasters_Publicity_Photo.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","grp":"chart","list_year":null,"rank":17,"year":1959,"note":null,"total":6841,"scaruffi":null},{"id":245563783135764,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1959","src":"singles","grp":"billboard_year_end","list_year":1959,"rank":17,"year":1959,"note":"","total":100,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This song was widely referred to as the follow-up to \"Yakati Yak,\" although \"The Shadow Knows\" was released prior. It took significantly longer to write than \"Yakati Yak,\" with Max Stoller quickly coming up with appropriate music, but Jerry Lieber agonizing for weeks before finding the title and basic lyric idea, which Stoller helped refine. The songwriters deliberately tried to replicate the sound of \"Yakati Yak.\" The song reached number two on the pop charts, making it almost as big a hit as \"Yakati Yak.\" \"Charlie Brown was almost as big a hit as Yakite Yak, reaching number two on the pop charts.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 68: \"Yakety Yak\" by the Coasters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dtMHDD0-oOo"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":218651595932715,"artist":"Coasters","artist_id":243657217553935,"title":"Charlie Brown","year":1959,"ess":0,"yt":"-D0SUGdO4ok","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scar